595	// --- Streaming root ----------------------------------------------------------
596	//
597	// The `root` command must hash batches too large (or made of records too
598	// long, or too numerous) to fit in memory. The construction below keeps:
599	//
600	//   * ONE fixed-size read buffer ([`READ_CAPACITY`] bytes), independently of
601	//     the file's total length — the file is never read whole;
602	//   * ONE leaf hash in progress, fed record bytes straight from that buffer,
603	//     independently of the longest record — a record is never stored;
604	//   * a stack of O(log n) finalized subtree hashes, independently of the
605	//     record count — leaf hashes are not retained.
606	//
607	// Nothing here bounds the input: memory does not shrink by refusing large
608	// files, long records or many records, only by never holding them.
609	//
610	// Result equivalence with the in-memory [`build_tree`] comes from the same
611	// RFC 6962 fold order: pushing leaf hashes left to right and merging equal
612	// levels builds complete subtrees in postfix order, and the final
613	// right-to-left fold joins exactly the sibling pairs the recursive
614	// definition's "largest power of two below n" split chooses. Uneven batches
615	// therefore need no duplicate of the last leaf and no padded empty leaf.
616	
617	/// Fixed capacity of the single read buffer used while streaming a batch.
618	/// Reads may return fewer bytes at any time (including one); the result is
619	/// independent of where read boundaries fall because records are recognized by
620	/// their LF bytes, not by chunk boundaries.
621	#[doc(hidden)]
622	pub const READ_CAPACITY: usize = 64 * 1024;
623	
624	/// Streaming RFC 6962 Merkle Tree Hash builder.
625	///
626	/// Feed the raw file bytes in arbitrary chunking with [`StreamRoot::extend`]
627	/// and finish with [`StreamRoot::finish`]. Bytes are split into records on LF
628	/// exactly like [`split_records`]: the LF is a separator and never content, a
629	/// trailing LF only terminates the last record, and an empty input is zero
630	/// records. All other bytes — spaces, tabs, CR, NUL, non-UTF-8 sequences —
631	/// are record content.
632	///
633	/// The builder stores a leaf hash in progress (a [`Sha256`] state) and a
634	/// log-depth stack of finalized subtree hashes; record bytes are fed straight
635	/// from the caller's read buffer, so no file-sized, record-sized or
636	/// record-count-sized storage is ever held. Its working memory is bounded
637	/// independently of the file's total size, of its longest record and of its
638	/// record count.
639	struct StreamRoot {
640	    /// Hash of the record currently being read: SHA-256(0x00 || record...).
641	    /// `None` between records; becoming `Some` writes the 0x00 leaf prefix
642	    /// exactly once, including for an empty record.
643	    leaf: Option<Sha256>,
644	    /// Level stack, bottom level first. An entry is the root hash of a
645	    /// complete finalized subtree together with its power-of-two leaf count
646	    /// (encoded as the level, 0 = a single leaf). Counts stay implicit in the
647	    /// level numbers, so nothing per record is retained.
648	    stack: Vec<StackNode>,
649	    /// Number of records finalized onto the stack so far. Used only to merge
650	    /// equal levels on push and to check the final fold.
651	    count: u64,
652	}
653	
654	/// One finalized subtree on [`StreamRoot`]'s stack.
655	#[derive(Clone, Copy)]
656	struct StackNode {
657	    level: u32,
658	    hash: [u8; 32],
659	}
